1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:07,040
An oil rig capsizes, sending 84 men to their frigid, watery graves.

2
00:00:07,860 --> 00:00:11,900
A jet fuel plant explodes, raining devastation for miles around.

3
00:00:12,660 --> 00:00:16,240
A pro golf star's plane goes down, shrouded in mystery.

4
00:00:18,060 --> 00:00:23,280
Four vehicles plunge into a river as a bridge collapses in the darkness of night.

5
00:00:23,840 --> 00:00:29,100
And the worst dam failure of the 19th century annihilates an entire town.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:34,020
Now, Engineering Disasters on Modern Marvels.

7
00:00:44,380 --> 00:00:49,840
Just ten miles outside the city of Las Vegas lies the suburb of Henderson, Nevada.

8
00:00:52,760 --> 00:00:57,780
On May 4th, 1988, this small community changed forever.

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:11,624
Three miles from town, two major explosions measuring 3.0 and 3.5 on the Richter scale ripped through the desert locale and sent visible shockwaves across a 10-mile

10
00:01:11,625 --> 00:01:12,325
radius.

11
00:01:15,340 --> 00:01:20,320
Two people died. Over 300 people in the surrounding area were injured.

12
00:01:22,540 --> 00:01:25,560
Property damage was nearly $100 million.

13
00:01:25,561 --> 00:01:33,760
The two explosions originated at the Pacific Engineering Production Company of Nevada,

14
00:01:34,940 --> 00:01:36,680
otherwise known as Pepcon.

15
00:01:38,920 --> 00:01:40,965
Pepcon was one of only two U.S.

16
00:01:40,966 --> 00:01:45,780
manufacturers of ammonium perchlorate, an oxidizer for solid rocket fuel.

17
00:01:45,781 --> 00:01:54,320
AP, or the long name is ammonium perchlorate, is a salt.

18
00:01:55,020 --> 00:01:59,360
It's comprised of ammonium, which is nitrogen and hydrogen,

19
00:02:00,200 --> 00:02:03,840
and chlorate, which is comprised of chlorine and oxygen.

20
00:02:06,060 --> 00:02:10,420
This odorless white granular substance is not volatile if left alone.

21
00:02:10,900 --> 00:02:13,820
However, it becomes highly explosive if contaminated,

22
00:02:13,821 --> 00:02:16,300
or combined with any kind of fuel.

23
00:02:16,760 --> 00:02:21,600
To give you some perspective, ammonium perchlorate, which is 55% by weight oxygen,

24
00:02:22,100 --> 00:02:27,160
as compared to the air we breathe, which is only approximately 29% by weight oxygen.

25
00:02:27,400 --> 00:02:35,860
So it's richer in oxygen and therefore promotes combustion of most fuels more intensely.

26
00:02:37,580 --> 00:02:43,100
Ammonium perchlorate is used on a large scale in solid fuel rocket engines for NASA and the military.

27
00:02:43,820 --> 00:02:49,660
It's the chemical in rocket propellant that releases oxygen, essential in the combustion of fuel.

28
00:02:51,400 --> 00:02:55,620
One of the biggest customers for AP is NASA's Space Shuttle program.

29
00:02:56,840 --> 00:03:03,800
On January 28, 1986, the Space Shuttle Challenger exploded 73 seconds after takeoff,

30
00:03:04,200 --> 00:03:06,580
causing NASA to ground all Space Shuttle flights.

31
00:03:08,500 --> 00:03:11,760
But PEPCON continued to manufacture ammonium perchlorate.

32
00:03:11,761 --> 00:03:14,560
and stockpiling.

33
00:03:16,060 --> 00:03:20,460
Never before had so much ammonium perchlorate been stored in one location.

34
00:03:21,380 --> 00:03:25,440
We had approximately 10.5 million pounds of ammonium perchlorate

35
00:03:25,441 --> 00:03:27,500
being stored at the Pepcon facility.

36
00:03:28,920 --> 00:03:32,780
Pepcon was multiple buildings occupying approximately 15 acres.

37
00:03:32,781 --> 00:03:38,800
The buildings were typically constructed of fiberglass-reinforced plastic panels

38
00:03:38,801 --> 00:03:45,160
over metal purlins and beams and posts.

39
00:03:46,420 --> 00:03:51,420
This was done because of the nature of perchlorate being so corrosive.

40
00:03:51,840 --> 00:03:56,720
On the morning of May 4, 1988, an open barrel of AP caught fire.

41
00:03:56,960 --> 00:04:00,960
It's our observation that the plant housekeeping was not very good.

42
00:04:01,300 --> 00:04:05,000
There's a very strong possibility that much of the ammonium perchlorate on site

43
00:04:05,001 --> 00:04:07,460
had some very low level of contamination.

44
00:04:07,461 --> 00:04:14,460
The fire from the one barrel rapidly spread to other storage containers and the fiberglass structure itself.

45
00:04:14,461 --> 00:04:19,460
The only tool that workers had to combat the fire was a garden hose.

46
00:04:19,461 --> 00:04:21,460
It was ineffectual.

47
00:04:21,461 --> 00:04:28,460
With a class 4 oxidizer, such as ammonium perchlorate, there is no real method of fire extinguishment.

48
00:04:28,461 --> 00:04:34,460
Once it gets going, you can't put enough water on it quickly enough to break the combustion chain.

49
00:04:34,461 --> 00:04:35,161
chain.

50
00:04:35,162 --> 00:04:40,780
The employees at PEPCON, our analysis showed and follow-up investigations show that

51
00:04:40,781 --> 00:04:48,120
the safety briefings that they had basically included if there's a fire in the plant run like

52
00:04:48,121 --> 00:04:53,702
hell. With wind blowing, the fire quickly became a raging inferno.

53
00:04:53,703 --> 00:04:56,240
When subjected to heat, the AP

54
00:04:56,241 --> 00:05:00,361
started releasing its energy, goes into a self-heating process.

55
00:05:00,362 --> 00:05:01,800
It's releasing copious

56
00:05:01,801 --> 00:05:05,560
amounts of oxygen and it reaches what's called critical mass.

57
00:05:05,561 --> 00:05:11,680
At just a few hundred degrees, AP can begin to consume itself, acting as its own oxidizer

58
00:05:11,681 --> 00:05:12,381
and fuel.

59
00:05:14,380 --> 00:05:16,320
A chain reaction began.

60
00:05:17,120 --> 00:05:18,900
The plant was being engulfed by fire.

61
00:05:21,300 --> 00:05:26,500
Polyethylene drums launched like rockets into other nearby storage areas and ignited

62
00:05:26,501 --> 00:05:27,201
more AP.

63
00:05:28,540 --> 00:05:31,280
Detonation is a high-speed explosion.

64
00:05:31,800 --> 00:05:38,040
as opposed to just a fire or a deflagration, which is a low-speed explosion.

65
00:05:38,180 --> 00:05:44,760
What we had at PEPCON was a detonation because it caused that final shock wave.

66
00:05:45,200 --> 00:05:48,040
The fire burned for approximately 20 minutes,

67
00:05:48,780 --> 00:05:53,460
generating tremendous amounts of heat before the explosion that caused the first shock wave.

68
00:05:55,820 --> 00:05:57,080
That's going to be loud.

69
00:05:57,081 --> 00:06:04,000
It basically proceeded through the storage of ammonium perchlorate, very much like a Roman candle.

70
00:06:06,820 --> 00:06:12,480
And then this very rapid heating and thermal shock caused the ammonium perchlorate to explode.

71
00:06:14,380 --> 00:06:16,860
A total of seven explosions occurred.

72
00:06:16,861 --> 00:06:27,120
Firefighters rushing to the scene were caught in the shockwave and reassessed their adversary.

73
00:06:28,200 --> 00:06:32,720
As we were driving down Boulder Highway, I had the window open and one of the other explosions that occurred.

74
00:06:32,980 --> 00:06:37,900
I could actually feel it hit me, not that it did any damage, but I could feel the vibration of the explosion.

75
00:06:38,980 --> 00:06:44,820
To exacerbate an already critical situation, a natural gas pipeline ran directly under the plant.

76
00:06:46,860 --> 00:06:54,520
The final detonation, which measured about 3.5 on the Richter scale,

77
00:06:54,940 --> 00:06:57,760
occurred right over the gas line.

78
00:06:57,820 --> 00:07:01,460
It was a 16-inch gas line servicing the city of Las Vegas, Nevada.

79
00:07:02,280 --> 00:07:06,980
Having consumed its fuel of AP, the initial fire burned itself out,

80
00:07:07,420 --> 00:07:10,820
but the ruptured pipeline fed a new 100-foot bloom of fire.

81
00:07:11,940 --> 00:07:15,220
It burned for almost an hour before the line was shut down.

82
00:07:15,221 --> 00:07:24,678
There was a lot of speculation in the early part of the investigation that the pipeline may have played a role in at least initiating or aiding and abetting those

83
00:07:24,679 --> 00:07:25,379
explosions.

84
00:07:26,220 --> 00:07:31,180
It took months of investigation by teams of experts to determine the cause of the explosions.

85
00:07:31,900 --> 00:07:39,620
We were retained to bring in the appropriate people with the right technologies to understand what went wrong and how it happened.

86
00:07:39,621 --> 00:07:48,040
What investigators surmised was that welding debris had drifted through the air to an area of open barrels, igniting the AP.

87
00:07:48,460 --> 00:07:58,120
What we found out was that there had been welding and what's called flame-cutting activities on some of the steel structure in the batch-dry building.

88
00:07:58,980 --> 00:08:08,460
Open barrels of AP were common in certain areas of the plant, but that meant they were exposed to dust, fibers, and other contaminants, creating the perfect tinder.

89
00:08:09,100 --> 00:08:14,080
The storage containers that held the AP also contributed to the intense heat and combustion.

90
00:08:14,960 --> 00:08:19,620
They chose to store the ammonia plurie in several different kinds of containers.

91
00:08:20,680 --> 00:08:24,260
One container they used was what we call an aluminum tote bin.

92
00:08:24,860 --> 00:08:30,340
Another container used was what we call poly bins, and it's like a smaller 55-gallon drum.

93
00:08:30,341 --> 00:08:34,820
Those two types of containers were the majority of the containers.

94
00:08:34,821 --> 00:08:43,660
Now, the irony of the situation is that one of the standard formulas for a solid rocket propellant

95
00:08:43,661 --> 00:08:53,100
is approximately 70% by weight ammonium perchlorate, 15% by weight aluminum particles, and 15% by weight rubber binder.

96
00:08:53,640 --> 00:08:59,580
The polyethylene drums provided the rubber binder, and the other bins provided the aluminum fuel.

97
00:09:00,360 --> 00:09:04,060
And when that's all mixed together, it makes a solid propellant.

98
00:09:04,820 --> 00:09:16,240
after investigators examined the evidence left at the scene and conducted various experiments

99
00:09:16,241 --> 00:09:22,640
with ammonium perchlorate the reason for the explosions became clear the question was how

100
00:09:22,641 --> 00:09:28,440
can ammonium perchlorate detonate the answer is it can detonate when it's heated and very

101
00:09:28,441 --> 00:09:34,280
definitely it was heated intensely by the precursor fires here those fires being particularly

102
00:09:34,281 --> 00:09:39,700
intense because they were fueled by ammonium perchlorate acting as an oxidizer.

103
00:09:45,040 --> 00:09:50,720
The Pepcon catastrophe demonstrated the extreme importance of proper AP storage.

104
00:09:51,280 --> 00:09:55,840
I think there's a lot of things that we learned after the disaster, and certainly the most

105
00:09:55,841 --> 00:10:01,240
important is a safety culture within the organization that's manufacturing these types

106
00:10:01,241 --> 00:10:05,400
of chemicals and remember that this stuff was basically stored out in the parking lot.

107
00:10:07,480 --> 00:10:15,400
Many, many thousands of pounds of it stored in close proximity and we know for any kind of

108
00:10:16,200 --> 00:10:21,960
substance that's potentially explosive that that's bad practice that instead small amounts

109
00:10:21,961 --> 00:10:27,720
should be stored and compartmentalized with cement walls or something such that if one area

110
00:10:27,721 --> 00:10:31,880
does ignite and go, it's not going to sympathetically detonate all the rest.

111
00:10:33,880 --> 00:10:39,520
Pacific Engineering Company of Nevada has since changed its name to Western Electrochemical

112
00:10:39,521 --> 00:10:43,408
and is relocated to an even more remote location.

113
00:10:43,409 --> 00:10:46,820
However, now it is the sole manufacturer of

114
00:10:46,821 --> 00:10:49,031
AP in North America.

115
00:10:49,032 --> 00:10:56,660
The AP explosion measuring magnitude 3.5 on the Richter scale equaled

116
00:10:56,661 --> 00:11:01,060
the energy yield of over 300 tons of TNT.

117
00:11:01,061 --> 00:11:04,340
Engineering disasters will return on Modern Marvels.

118
00:11:09,300 --> 00:11:13,560
Each day, about 90,000 vehicles travel Interstate 95

119
00:11:13,561 --> 00:11:15,720
between Connecticut and New York.

120
00:11:15,721 --> 00:11:20,720
But on June 28, 1983, four such vehicles wouldn't make it home.

121
00:11:20,721 --> 00:11:28,840
At 1.28 a.m., a 100-foot section of a Connecticut turnpike bridge collapsed,

122
00:11:29,360 --> 00:11:35,960
sending two cars and two tractor trailers plummeting 70 feet into the Mianus River below.

123
00:11:36,540 --> 00:11:38,800
The first truck immediately locked up its brakes.

124
00:11:38,920 --> 00:11:45,080
That was the one in the right lane and started skinning about 150 feet before the beginning of the structure.

125
00:11:46,020 --> 00:11:51,000
At that point, the person following did in fact slam on their brakes also,

126
00:11:51,140 --> 00:11:54,700
fearing an accident because of the jackknifing trucks in front of them,

127
00:11:55,120 --> 00:11:57,740
at which point three vehicles disappeared from sight.

128
00:11:59,860 --> 00:12:02,560
I reached the site and I looked over the edge

129
00:12:02,561 --> 00:12:05,400
and I thought I was in some sort of a science fiction movie.

130
00:12:05,740 --> 00:12:08,800
There was lights all over the place, police lights, sirens.

131
00:12:09,520 --> 00:12:13,200
And I looked over the bridge and there were two trucks dangling in the water.

132
00:12:13,201 --> 00:12:17,980
there was debris from the bridge and there was all kinds of debris from the trucks and the first

133
00:12:17,981 --> 00:12:26,120
thought in my mind was that only four cars fell over lives have been lost and a major artery in

134
00:12:26,121 --> 00:12:33,640
the transportation system was now severed by 95 was closed in both directions as national guard

135
00:12:33,641 --> 00:12:40,780
helicopters helped police reroute traffic state and federal agencies as well as private engineering

136
00:12:40,781 --> 00:12:45,840
firms were called in to investigate the accident that devastated the affluent community of

137
00:12:45,841 --> 00:12:46,541
Greenwich, Connecticut.

138
00:12:49,380 --> 00:12:52,380
It had been almost 30 years since the bridge's construction.

139
00:12:53,480 --> 00:12:58,980
As the federal interstate program of the 1950s drove the northeastern United States into

140
00:12:58,981 --> 00:13:04,140
the superhighway age, bridges and highways were being built with unprecedented speed

141
00:13:04,141 --> 00:13:05,360
and determination.

142
00:13:05,361 --> 00:13:12,360
To keep costs in check, economical methods of construction were used.

143
00:13:12,361 --> 00:13:18,460
The Mianus River Bridge, opened in 1958, used a construction technique known as the pin

144
00:13:18,461 --> 00:13:24,580
and hanger design to connect the bridge's roadway to its supports.

145
00:13:24,581 --> 00:13:31,800
A pin and hanger assembly consists of two steel plates attached to girders by two steel pins.

146
00:13:31,801 --> 00:13:36,260
Retaining bolts are then installed through the pins with a keeper plate at each end.

147
00:13:37,360 --> 00:13:40,120
A standard nut is fastened to complete the assembly.

148
00:13:40,920 --> 00:13:46,440
The suspended span of the Mianus River Bridge had two of these assemblies, one at each corner of the north end.

149
00:13:47,080 --> 00:13:55,960
And it allows the structure to move back and forth because with long spans you have issues with expansion and contraction, especially in the environment in Connecticut.

150
00:13:55,961 --> 00:14:02,960
The bridge's location, years of heavy traffic, and harsh weather conditions all took a severe toll.

151
00:14:02,961 --> 00:14:13,960
You're over a water course that happens to be a salt martian, fundamentally subject to tidal flow from Long Island Sound.

152
00:14:13,961 --> 00:14:18,960
But also the material that we put on the roads in the winter has quite a bit of salt in it.

153
00:14:18,961 --> 00:14:19,661
in it.

154
00:14:19,662 --> 00:14:26,200
And over a course of years, recognizing this is now 30 years old practically, rust built up.

155
00:14:28,400 --> 00:14:33,680
The official statement from investigating engineers was that the Mianus River bridge

156
00:14:33,681 --> 00:14:39,200
collapsed because of undetected lateral displacement of the hangars by corrosion-induced forces.

157
00:14:42,320 --> 00:14:46,760
The rust caused the inside hangar of the northeast corner of the suspended span

158
00:14:46,761 --> 00:14:49,260
to slide off the lower pin.

159
00:14:50,160 --> 00:14:52,700
Now the entire weight of the span's north end

160
00:14:52,701 --> 00:14:55,660
rested precariously on one hanger assembly.

161
00:14:57,060 --> 00:14:59,620
Because it wasn't designed to carry this much weight,

162
00:15:00,340 --> 00:15:03,140
it was only a matter of time before it too failed.

163
00:15:03,900 --> 00:15:07,800
And then ultimately the pin worked its way out of the remaining hanger

164
00:15:07,801 --> 00:15:09,900
and that corner of the span fell.

165
00:15:11,820 --> 00:15:15,300
While rust corrosion of steel subjected to this type of environment

166
00:15:15,301 --> 00:15:18,000
is essentially a natural, unavoidable process.

167
00:15:18,880 --> 00:15:22,100
The undetected part was definitely avoidable.

168
00:15:24,300 --> 00:15:27,620
Hardesty in Hanover was one of the engineering firms

169
00:15:27,621 --> 00:15:29,340
called in to evaluate the collapse.

170
00:15:30,480 --> 00:15:32,300
After looking at the situation,

171
00:15:32,560 --> 00:15:37,300
we knew that the other three spans were in immediate danger.

172
00:15:37,940 --> 00:15:40,060
And by noon the next day,

173
00:15:40,061 --> 00:15:46,220
we had a mechanism to clamp the remaining pin and hangers together

174
00:15:46,221 --> 00:15:50,120
so that they would resist failure.

175
00:15:50,121 --> 00:15:54,200
A temporary bridge span was installed 25 days after the accident,

176
00:15:54,920 --> 00:15:56,260
allowing traffic to resume.

177
00:15:57,280 --> 00:15:59,720
And construction began on a more permanent structure,

178
00:16:00,240 --> 00:16:02,220
which opened in January of 1984.

179
00:16:02,980 --> 00:16:06,720
But with so many of the state's bridges built with the same failed technique,

180
00:16:06,721 --> 00:16:11,220
The race to prevent more potentially fatal bridge collapses was on.

181
00:16:12,720 --> 00:16:15,380
Subsequent to the Bionis River event,

182
00:16:16,700 --> 00:16:20,380
not only the state of Connecticut, but the entire country went out

183
00:16:20,381 --> 00:16:23,080
and started inspecting all their pin and hanger assemblies.

184
00:16:23,740 --> 00:16:27,380
We've inspected all the hanger assemblies, cleaned all the hanger assemblies,

185
00:16:27,840 --> 00:16:32,340
upgraded the hanger assemblies to be certain that they had the appropriate connection pieces,

186
00:16:32,920 --> 00:16:35,300
and then we put in what we call catcher mitts,

187
00:16:35,301 --> 00:16:40,440
a structural component beneath the hangar that if the hangar does in fact fail,

188
00:16:41,020 --> 00:16:42,840
that end of the structure can't fall.

189
00:16:43,720 --> 00:16:48,780
The accident drew national attention to the problems caused by deferred bridge maintenance.

190
00:16:51,760 --> 00:16:56,220
Connecticut's Governor William O'Neill embarked on an unprecedented 10-year,

191
00:16:56,400 --> 00:17:01,780
$5.5 billion plan to reconstruct and rehabilitate the state's roads and bridges.

192
00:17:05,300 --> 00:17:12,220
In 1989, the Mianus Bridge was transformed again, with expanded traffic lanes and updated retrofitting.

193
00:17:13,360 --> 00:17:20,320
Thanks to Governor O'Neill's dedication, Connecticut now boasts the nation's best bridge inspection and maintenance program.

194
00:17:22,800 --> 00:17:29,300
Perhaps the bridge designers of the 1950s didn't anticipate that future generations would neglect maintenance.

195
00:17:29,301 --> 00:17:35,960
But the three lives taken by the failure of the Mianus River Bridge may have saved many more.

196
00:17:37,460 --> 00:17:44,340
In 1983, the Mianus River Bridge was secured and rebuilt at a cost of over $75 million.

197
00:17:45,400 --> 00:17:49,300
Engineering disasters will return on Modern Marvels.

198
00:17:54,800 --> 00:17:58,100
Tumultuous storms in the North Atlantic are not uncommon.

199
00:17:59,300 --> 00:18:04,680
But the Ocean Ranger was built to withstand the worst of them.

200
00:18:06,680 --> 00:18:13,020
Owned by Ocean Drilling and Exploration Company, or Odeco, it was hailed as unsinkable.

201
00:18:14,140 --> 00:18:20,200
The $120 million semi-submersible oil rig was the pride of an offshore exploration fleet.

202
00:18:21,220 --> 00:18:28,040
Its job was to search for oil farther and deeper than ever before, in areas previously thought too hostile.

203
00:18:28,041 --> 00:18:35,040
A semi-submersible is a particular type of vessel that's really been adapted exclusively for drilling offshore.

204
00:18:35,041 --> 00:18:41,040
And the reason for that is it's got particularly good motion characteristics in waves.

205
00:18:41,041 --> 00:18:45,040
And that is achieved by having the pontoons underneath the water.

206
00:18:45,041 --> 00:18:47,040
The main part of the buoyancy is underneath the surface.

207
00:18:47,041 --> 00:18:52,040
And the part that crosses the surface are the columns, which are relatively small in area.

208
00:18:52,041 --> 00:18:57,040
And what this means is that the vessel doesn't respond to wave action very much.

209
00:18:57,041 --> 00:19:00,040
much and so the motion characteristics are good for drilling.

210
00:19:00,041 --> 00:19:06,040
With a platform the size of two football fields and towering 35 stories from Kiel to

211
00:19:06,041 --> 00:19:08,860
Derrick, it was the symbol of stability.

212
00:19:08,861 --> 00:19:13,420
The Ocean Ranger at the time was built was the largest of such vessel in the world.

213
00:19:13,421 --> 00:19:19,920
It had two pontoons and eight columns which was not unusual, but it was the largest vessel

214
00:19:19,921 --> 00:19:25,200
and generally with vessels the larger they are the better they behave in the sea.

215
00:19:25,201 --> 00:19:30,380
So it was probably the most tolerant vessel of its time.

216
00:19:30,381 --> 00:19:34,680
And of course at the time it was probably the most modern.

217
00:19:34,681 --> 00:19:41,380
In November 1980, the Ocean Ranger arrived on the Grand Banks, 170 miles off the coast

218
00:19:41,381 --> 00:19:42,380
of Newfoundland.

219
00:19:42,381 --> 00:19:49,360
Yeah, back in the 80s when work wasn't so plenty, you know, when the Eudrichs came to

220
00:19:49,361 --> 00:19:50,920
Newfoundland it was great.

221
00:19:50,921 --> 00:19:56,060
I mean, people got jobs offshore and they went out there and they made good money.

222
00:19:56,880 --> 00:19:57,740
And it was good work.

223
00:19:58,100 --> 00:20:01,040
The oil business in Newfoundland put a lot of people on their feet.

224
00:20:03,760 --> 00:20:09,060
On February 6, 1982, the crew of the Ocean Ranger got its first scare

225
00:20:09,061 --> 00:20:14,260
when a problem with the ballast control caused the rig to tilt or list unexpectedly.

226
00:20:15,400 --> 00:20:18,180
Ballast control is the process by which the rig is kept level

227
00:20:18,181 --> 00:20:23,460
by pumping seawater into or out of the ballast tanks located in the pontoons.

228
00:20:23,760 --> 00:20:29,600
I was in the crane moving around some stuff and the rigs started to list.

229
00:20:29,900 --> 00:20:31,900
So we didn't know what the hell was going on there.

230
00:20:32,140 --> 00:20:39,100
So I got down out of the crane and my workers and myself and all the other people went to the light bulbs.

231
00:20:39,101 --> 00:20:43,620
And it was kind of panicking because we didn't know it was a beautiful day.

232
00:20:43,621 --> 00:20:47,080
and if it had to be a bad day that day,

233
00:20:47,140 --> 00:20:48,480
I wouldn't want to be aboard that rig.

234
00:20:49,300 --> 00:20:50,880
Luckily, the seas were calm

235
00:20:50,881 --> 00:20:52,540
and the problem was corrected,

236
00:20:52,860 --> 00:20:55,060
but the stage for catastrophe was set.

237
00:20:57,100 --> 00:20:59,140
John Crow went on his monthly shore leave

238
00:20:59,141 --> 00:21:00,100
a few days later.

239
00:21:03,420 --> 00:21:05,680
On February 14, 1982,

240
00:21:06,380 --> 00:21:08,420
an intense storm hit the Grand Banks

241
00:21:08,421 --> 00:21:09,840
with winds of 90 knots

242
00:21:09,841 --> 00:21:11,580
and waves as high as 50 feet.

243
00:21:13,620 --> 00:21:18,680
At about 8 p.m., a violent wave broke through a porthole in the ballast control room

244
00:21:18,681 --> 00:21:24,260
and washed over the control panel, causing the system to short-circuit.

245
00:21:25,200 --> 00:21:33,520
Now you have a whole bunch of electric components that are soaking wet with not just water, salt water.

246
00:21:34,000 --> 00:21:40,480
So, of course, immediately the whole electrical system controlling the stability of the Ocean Ranger falls apart.

247
00:21:40,481 --> 00:21:44,500
I mean, stops working, starts working effortlessly.

248
00:21:45,460 --> 00:21:50,560
Because such emergency situations were never anticipated on the unsinkable Ocean Ranger,

249
00:21:51,140 --> 00:21:53,500
technical and safety training had been less than adequate.

250
00:21:54,340 --> 00:21:59,200
The confused crew scrambled to fix the problem, but this time the seas were raging.

251
00:22:00,780 --> 00:22:06,740
Seawater entered the forward tanks in the port pontoon and caused a rapid 10-degree list to port and forward.

252
00:22:07,060 --> 00:22:10,160
The rig wasn't designed to deal with this kind of situation.

253
00:22:10,480 --> 00:22:15,100
Where they had difficulties because in the Ocean Ranger, the ballast pumps were located in the stern.

254
00:22:15,580 --> 00:22:16,760
It was listed to the bow.

255
00:22:17,460 --> 00:22:20,580
These pumps were not capable of pumping out the bow tanks.

256
00:22:21,660 --> 00:22:26,200
The crew was also untrained in the operation of the pneumatic manual override system.

257
00:22:27,420 --> 00:22:33,500
No one on that ship knew how to use extra long threaded control rods,

258
00:22:33,501 --> 00:22:38,440
which would bypass the control panel that was now soaked altogether

259
00:22:38,441 --> 00:22:41,960
and could cause them to manually turn on the air relays

260
00:22:41,961 --> 00:22:45,000
that would change the valving and the stability of the ship.

261
00:22:47,280 --> 00:22:52,120
Just after 1 a.m. on February 15th, a distress call was issued.

262
00:22:53,740 --> 00:22:55,060
Search and rescue, Bairns.

263
00:22:55,160 --> 00:22:56,220
Mobile radio calling?

264
00:22:56,420 --> 00:22:57,120
Yeah.

265
00:22:57,121 --> 00:22:58,500
Got an emergency on the Ocean Ranger.

266
00:22:58,960 --> 00:23:01,680
Half an hour later, the crew commenced evacuation.

267
00:23:01,940 --> 00:23:03,880
They want to evacuate by the looks of it.

268
00:23:03,881 --> 00:23:09,960
But only a fraction of the men were able to board the lifeboats,

269
00:23:10,360 --> 00:23:13,960
which weren't even designed for violent storms in heavy listing situations.

270
00:23:14,500 --> 00:23:16,700
These circumstances had not been anticipated.

271
00:23:17,180 --> 00:23:20,600
The launch method where you just drop them on two cables,

272
00:23:21,060 --> 00:23:24,660
you really have very little control over what happens as soon as you start that launch.

273
00:23:25,420 --> 00:23:28,140
The rig capsized at 3.15 a.m.

274
00:23:29,780 --> 00:23:33,260
A nearby supply ship attempted to carry out rescue operations.

275
00:23:33,880 --> 00:23:39,080
but the seas were too rough and too cold

276
00:23:39,081 --> 00:23:43,640
wearing only their clothes and life jackets the men including those who fell

277
00:23:43,641 --> 00:23:47,960
out of the lifeboats were immobilized almost instantly and couldn't grasp the

278
00:23:47,961 --> 00:23:51,000
lines being cast

279
00:23:51,001 --> 00:23:55,480
all 84 of the ocean rangers men met their watery graves in a matter of

280
00:23:55,481 --> 00:23:59,640
minutes

281
00:23:59,641 --> 00:24:01,641
With no survivors, Canadian and U.S.

282
00:24:01,642 --> 00:24:05,920
investigators had to somehow piece together what happened that fateful night.

283
00:24:10,900 --> 00:24:14,820
Canada's National Research Council built a scale model of the Ocean Ranger

284
00:24:14,821 --> 00:24:18,660
and performed a myriad of lab tests to determine what went wrong.

285
00:24:19,600 --> 00:24:28,360
This is the model of the Ocean Ranger that was used as part of the inquiry into the disaster conducted by the Royal Commission.

286
00:24:28,361 --> 00:24:31,500
This area here is where the ballast control room was located.

287
00:24:31,620 --> 00:24:34,820
The portholes would have been located so that you could see the draft marks.

288
00:24:35,380 --> 00:24:37,320
The water level would have been about here.

289
00:24:38,480 --> 00:24:43,860
And so you can see that the control room is located a fair ways below the main deck here.

290
00:24:44,720 --> 00:24:47,640
The ballast control was located too low on the rig,

291
00:24:48,060 --> 00:24:52,040
and the control panel was too close to a porthole, rendering it vulnerable.

292
00:24:52,980 --> 00:24:56,860
When you consider ballast control panels and rig designs that have been made since,

293
00:24:56,861 --> 00:25:01,380
it is almost universal, or I'm sure it is universal now,

294
00:25:01,460 --> 00:25:04,260
that if they are not waterproof, then they are in situations

295
00:25:04,261 --> 00:25:09,500
where either water from the outside or water from internal pipes cannot get at them.

296
00:25:09,740 --> 00:25:11,760
Aside from identifying the design flaws,

297
00:25:12,820 --> 00:25:16,880
investigators cited inadequate crew training and numerous safety violations.

298
00:25:18,020 --> 00:25:20,320
As a result of the Ocean Ranger tragedy,

299
00:25:20,900 --> 00:25:23,900
survival suits, as well as technical and survival training,

300
00:25:23,901 --> 00:25:27,860
were made mandatory on rigs stationed in North Atlantic waters.

301
00:25:28,840 --> 00:25:30,760
Lifeboat launch systems have improved dramatically,

302
00:25:31,400 --> 00:25:36,200
and semi-submersibles are now required to carry lifeboats for 200% crew capacity,

303
00:25:36,680 --> 00:25:38,980
with half located on opposing poles of the vessel,

304
00:25:39,760 --> 00:25:41,660
in case of a heavy list in either direction.

305
00:25:43,140 --> 00:25:47,860
On the Ocean Ranger on that fatal night, I mean, I guess the weather was so bad,

306
00:25:47,861 --> 00:25:53,940
and I guess if they had to close the dark window in the lake,

307
00:25:54,400 --> 00:25:58,140
you know, so many things you can say they could have done probably.

308
00:25:58,640 --> 00:26:02,540
And I guess Mother Nature just let everybody know

309
00:26:02,541 --> 00:26:04,860
that you can build nothing big enough that she can take.

310
00:26:09,740 --> 00:26:13,960
The Ocean Ranger weighed approximately 25,000 metric tons,

311
00:26:14,540 --> 00:26:16,360
about half the weight of the Titanic.

312
00:26:16,361 --> 00:26:21,360
Engineering Disasters will return on Modern Marvels.

313
00:26:24,360 --> 00:26:29,360
Johnstown, Pennsylvania, lies in the heart of the Allegheny Mountains.

314
00:26:30,360 --> 00:26:34,360
This 200-year-old town is a place of many stories.

315
00:26:34,361 --> 00:26:41,360
But the most famous and the most tragic is that of the Great Flood of 1889.

316
00:26:46,360 --> 00:26:59,460
Well, Johnstown was a sort of backwoods trading center when it was founded in 1800.

317
00:26:59,461 --> 00:27:05,420
It boomed when the Pennsylvania Canal came through in the 1830s, and then 20 years later

318
00:27:05,421 --> 00:27:07,760
when the Pennsylvania Railroad came through.

319
00:27:08,580 --> 00:27:09,700
And it was a matter of decades.

320
00:27:09,820 --> 00:27:14,500
It had gone from kind of a frontier outpost to one of the leading steel centers in the

321
00:27:14,501 --> 00:27:15,201
world.

322
00:27:16,360 --> 00:27:18,700
As industry grew, so did the town.

323
00:27:19,680 --> 00:27:23,220
Approximately 30,000 people resided in the area in 1889.

324
00:27:25,460 --> 00:27:28,300
Its location was a major reason for its success.

325
00:27:28,720 --> 00:27:33,300
There are three major rivers around the Johnstown area.

326
00:27:33,620 --> 00:27:38,140
You have the Little Connemaw River, and you have the Stony Creek River.

327
00:27:38,940 --> 00:27:43,680
And these two rivers merge to form the Connemaw River itself.

328
00:27:43,681 --> 00:27:49,700
These rivers have provided what was necessary for the industries that were here,

329
00:27:50,040 --> 00:27:53,640
and these rivers helped put the town on the map.

330
00:27:54,540 --> 00:28:00,540
The rivers also provided mass transportation and helped create the Pennsylvania Mainline Canal System.

331
00:28:01,260 --> 00:28:09,680
By 1834, you had this wonderfully ambitious system that went basically from Pittsburgh to Philadelphia

332
00:28:09,681 --> 00:28:14,680
Philadelphia by a combination of narrow gauge railroad and traditional canals.

333
00:28:15,680 --> 00:28:19,680
One problem with this system was a lack of water during the summer months.

334
00:28:19,681 --> 00:28:26,680
To ensure maximum mobility, the answer was to build a dam that would hold a reservoir of water to be channeled into the canals.

335
00:28:30,680 --> 00:28:35,680
The site chosen was 14 miles east of and 410 feet above Johnstown.

336
00:28:35,681 --> 00:28:40,680
construction began on the South Fork Dam in 1838.

337
00:28:40,681 --> 00:28:47,680
It was made of puddled earth and what that means is they would just make a layer of earth, dirt and rock.

338
00:28:47,681 --> 00:28:51,680
Water would be added to make it airtight and watertight.

339
00:28:51,681 --> 00:28:56,680
The center of the embankment consisted of slate, stones and more earth.

340
00:28:56,681 --> 00:29:01,680
The downstream portion was built up mostly of rocks, weighing up to 10 tons.

341
00:29:01,681 --> 00:29:07,460
It had, we believe, certainly one spillway, but we think it had a second emergency spillway.

342
00:29:07,740 --> 00:29:15,920
It had pipes running underneath it, a culvert, that allowed the operator of the dam to actually drain it if there was an emergency.

343
00:29:16,680 --> 00:29:27,640
Taking 14 years to complete, the dam stood 72 feet high, 918 feet across, 10 feet thick at the top, and 220 feet thick at its base.

344
00:29:28,180 --> 00:29:29,540
It seemed impenetrable.

345
00:29:29,541 --> 00:29:40,129
But one of the many ironies in the timeline of the Johnstown flood story was that the western reservoir of the Pennsylvania Mainline Canal was finished just in time

346
00:29:40,130 --> 00:29:42,260
for canals to go out of business.

347
00:29:43,900 --> 00:29:47,740
The railroads had taken over as the preferred source of transportation.

348
00:29:48,660 --> 00:29:53,660
With no need for a reservoir, the dam stood in neglect for the next 27 years.

349
00:29:53,661 --> 00:30:03,160
In 1862, a heavy storm caused a collapse in the center portion of the dam, which destroyed the stone culvert.

350
00:30:04,160 --> 00:30:13,780
The marred structure sat until 1879, when industrialist Benjamin Ruff bought the land and created the exclusive South Fork Fishing and Hunting Club.

351
00:30:14,080 --> 00:30:20,520
Ruff, who had worked for the railroad, hired some workmen and came up here and began to rebuild the dam.

352
00:30:20,521 --> 00:30:29,540
They had no engineer working on the project, and it was really just a construction gang, a railroad construction gang.

353
00:30:30,080 --> 00:30:33,680
Built like a railroad embankment, the dam was eventually repaired.

354
00:30:34,600 --> 00:30:39,080
But puddled clay was not used, and the culvert was not reinstalled.

355
00:30:39,880 --> 00:30:42,640
The old reservoir was now renamed Connemaw Lake.

356
00:30:43,780 --> 00:30:48,040
The South Fork Dam was really an accident waiting to happen.

357
00:30:48,041 --> 00:30:52,980
When it was reconstructed, there were several very serious engineering flaws,

358
00:30:53,420 --> 00:30:57,340
which really totally set the stage for disaster.

359
00:30:57,900 --> 00:31:04,360
Along with no discharge system, one of the most damaging changes was the placement of mesh screens at the spillway,

360
00:31:04,860 --> 00:31:07,900
which prevented fish that had been imported for the club from escaping.

361
00:31:09,920 --> 00:31:14,280
The dam stood for close to another decade, until 1889.

362
00:31:18,040 --> 00:31:22,020
the great storm of 1889 arrived.

363
00:31:23,520 --> 00:31:29,160
By the time people got up the next morning, on the morning of Friday, May 31st,

364
00:31:29,480 --> 00:31:34,520
many of the people in Johnstown saw that the streets in front of their homes were underwater.

365
00:31:35,260 --> 00:31:41,440
And it wasn't long before many of the streets in Johnstown actually resembled canals more than streets.

366
00:31:43,060 --> 00:31:46,020
The rivers surrounding Johnstown were overflowing.

367
00:31:46,960 --> 00:31:51,280
Connemaw Lake was taking on massive amounts of water in a very short period of time.

368
00:31:52,120 --> 00:31:58,140
Connemaw Lake would grow an estimated 30 to 33 percent larger than its normal size.

369
00:31:58,800 --> 00:32:04,880
Water was being dumped into the lake from the hills around it, as well as a lot of junk coming into the lake.

370
00:32:06,740 --> 00:32:11,060
Frantic efforts to stop water from flowing over the top of the dam were futile.

371
00:32:11,061 --> 00:32:16,020
The spillway's mesh screens were so choked with debris, they couldn't be removed.

372
00:32:16,300 --> 00:32:19,600
Water was rising about a foot an hour behind the dam.

373
00:32:19,820 --> 00:32:24,380
It was just a matter of time until water started over the top.

374
00:32:25,240 --> 00:32:30,940
Many railway travelers and residents of the towns that lined the Little Connemaw River took to the hills.

375
00:32:31,980 --> 00:32:35,020
Those that didn't paid with their lives.

376
00:32:35,021 --> 00:32:41,900
They stood watching until about 10 minutes after 3 when the eroded dam just pushed away.

377
00:32:44,080 --> 00:32:52,180
And 20 million tons of water just leapt out of the reservoir and began 14-mile descent on Johnstown.

378
00:32:53,960 --> 00:32:58,500
The flood wave ripped through the valley, decimating everything in its path.

379
00:32:58,501 --> 00:33:03,980
So what you almost had was a ball of water rolling down this narrow, sluice-like valley.

380
00:33:03,981 --> 00:33:08,300
and it pushed ahead of it everything, trees, houses, boulders.

381
00:33:08,620 --> 00:33:11,360
It pushed 80-ton locomotives as far as a mile,

382
00:33:11,740 --> 00:33:14,760
snapped off three-foot diameter hardwood trees.

383
00:33:17,020 --> 00:33:19,560
Reportedly reaching heights of up to 50 feet,

384
00:33:19,980 --> 00:33:23,580
the wall hit downtown, severing buildings from their foundations.

385
00:33:24,700 --> 00:33:28,760
And as the flood came into town, it broke into three directions

386
00:33:28,761 --> 00:33:33,080
and you had three tidal waves come crashing into Johnstown.

387
00:33:33,980 --> 00:33:42,640
Residents were floating on their rooftops hoping for an escape.

388
00:33:43,600 --> 00:33:46,420
It took only 10 minutes for the town to be wiped out.

389
00:33:49,960 --> 00:33:55,780
The once bustling Johnstown, proudly built on the backs of hard labor, was annihilated.

390
00:33:57,480 --> 00:34:03,860
An estimated 30 acres of debris as deep as 40 feet accumulated at the stone railroad bridge.

391
00:34:03,980 --> 00:34:04,980
on the edge of town.

392
00:34:07,340 --> 00:34:10,400
The wreckage contained coal, kerosene, and lime,

393
00:34:10,840 --> 00:34:13,660
which soon ignited and became a raging inferno.

394
00:34:14,440 --> 00:34:16,840
The fire burned for three and a half days.

395
00:34:17,040 --> 00:34:19,600
As many as 80 people who survived the flood died

396
00:34:19,601 --> 00:34:22,880
in a funeral pyre at the Stone Bridge.

397
00:34:23,200 --> 00:34:25,540
The death toll from the Johnstown flood

398
00:34:25,541 --> 00:34:28,940
is recorded as 2,209.

399
00:34:29,580 --> 00:34:32,500
It was estimated about 20,000 were in areas

400
00:34:32,501 --> 00:34:33,880
that were actually hit by the flood.

401
00:34:33,980 --> 00:34:36,740
And so about one in ten perished.

402
00:34:37,660 --> 00:34:40,080
But the town itself did not perish.

403
00:34:41,320 --> 00:34:46,660
The Johnstown Flood started the first major civilian relief effort in American history.

404
00:34:46,860 --> 00:34:50,100
It's one of the most inspirational stories in American history.

405
00:34:51,060 --> 00:34:58,280
A new organization called the American Red Cross, led by founder Clara Barton, was on the scene in five days.

406
00:34:58,281 --> 00:35:04,580
During the next five months, the Red Cross began its journey towards the organization we know today.

407
00:35:05,180 --> 00:35:09,560
25,000 people were homeless. They all needed to be fed and clothed.

408
00:35:09,800 --> 00:35:17,340
And for weeks, people were dependent on commissaries living in tents or hovels, you know, constructed out of wreckage.

409
00:35:18,180 --> 00:35:21,700
With remarkable speed, Johnstown began to rebuild.

410
00:35:23,140 --> 00:35:26,080
Within six weeks, the mills were back in operation.

411
00:35:26,081 --> 00:35:31,080
Industry and the railroads had made commitments to rebuild.

412
00:35:31,081 --> 00:35:37,080
In Johnstown, their memorial was going to be a newer, bigger, better city.

413
00:35:37,081 --> 00:35:41,080
Victims blamed the South Fork Fishing and Hunting Club.

414
00:35:41,081 --> 00:35:45,080
But the courts deemed the disaster an act of God.

415
00:35:45,081 --> 00:35:48,080
The survivors received no legal compensation.

416
00:35:48,081 --> 00:35:55,080
compensation. Today, the dam site is part of the Johnstown Flood National Memorial,

417
00:35:55,880 --> 00:36:02,221
run by the National Park Service. The area is once again used for recreation.

418
00:36:02,222 --> 00:36:02,922
But just

419
00:36:03,960 --> 00:36:10,960
14 miles downriver, in the Grandview Cemetery, lie the bodies of 777 unidentified souls of

420
00:36:12,100 --> 00:36:15,840
the flood, victims of the dam's faulty engineering.

421
00:36:18,080 --> 00:36:25,080
The water from Connemaw Lake took less than an hour to flow 14 miles down the narrow valley.

422
00:36:25,081 --> 00:36:32,080
The amount of water was estimated to equal that flowing over Niagara Falls in 36 minutes.

423
00:36:32,081 --> 00:36:37,080
Engineering disasters will return on Modern Marvels.

424
00:36:39,080 --> 00:36:43,080
Millions of people travel by air every day.

425
00:36:43,081 --> 00:36:46,080
Most on large commercial airliners.

426
00:36:48,080 --> 00:36:53,080
Only a lucky handful can afford the luxury of private jet travel.

427
00:36:53,081 --> 00:36:58,080
But even the fortunate few can't always escape misfortune.

428
00:37:05,080 --> 00:37:17,080
On October 25, 1999, champion pro golfer Payne Stewart, three other passengers, and two pilots boarded a Learjet 35 in Orlando, Florida.

429
00:37:18,080 --> 00:37:20,560
They were destined for Love Field in Dallas, Texas,

430
00:37:21,180 --> 00:37:23,080
the site of John F. Kennedy's final landing,

431
00:37:23,520 --> 00:37:25,760
and the eventual swearing-in of Lyndon B. Johnson.

432
00:37:26,600 --> 00:37:30,400
The Lyra 35 is a rather small twin-engine business jet.

433
00:37:30,520 --> 00:37:33,240
In this case, it was used for on-demand air taxi purposes.

434
00:37:34,100 --> 00:37:39,320
It's quite fast, cruising at an average airspeed of about 500 miles an hour.

435
00:37:39,980 --> 00:37:41,220
It's got long legs to it.

436
00:37:41,640 --> 00:37:44,840
You can go over 2,000 miles in one of these before you have to refuel.

437
00:37:44,841 --> 00:37:52,100
The airplane in question that Payne Stewart was flying, I believe, was built in 1976.

438
00:37:52,920 --> 00:37:55,880
It's been a very popular airplane, very reliable.

439
00:37:57,340 --> 00:37:59,920
The planned flight time was two hours.

440
00:38:01,140 --> 00:38:07,260
The plane was filled to 5,300 pounds fuel weight, enough for a four-hour, 45-minute flight.

441
00:38:08,320 --> 00:38:11,580
The aircraft departed at 9.19 a.m.

442
00:38:12,280 --> 00:38:14,720
Minutes later, something happened.

443
00:38:14,840 --> 00:38:21,480
They received a clearance up to 23,000 feet at about 927, I believe.

444
00:38:22,360 --> 00:38:25,860
They acknowledged this particular transmission without any problems.

445
00:38:26,040 --> 00:38:28,500
There was nothing excited in the pilot's voices at all.

446
00:38:29,420 --> 00:38:32,180
And they began to climb higher.

447
00:38:35,060 --> 00:38:37,820
Later, they were cleared to 39,000 feet,

448
00:38:38,380 --> 00:38:42,480
and the air traffic controller, about six minutes after this last transmission,

449
00:38:42,481 --> 00:38:45,840
called them up again to ask them to re-channelize their radio

450
00:38:45,841 --> 00:38:48,760
and transfer to another air route traffic control center.

451
00:38:50,160 --> 00:38:53,640
Well, they got no response, and he tried about five more times,

452
00:38:54,180 --> 00:38:55,420
still didn't get a response.

453
00:38:56,160 --> 00:38:58,260
The aircraft proceeded to climb an altitude

454
00:38:58,261 --> 00:39:02,520
and then deviated from its flight plan as it crossed into the central time zone.

455
00:39:03,200 --> 00:39:05,440
And then when it finally climbed through its altitude,

456
00:39:05,580 --> 00:39:07,700
its assigned altitude, without leveling off,

457
00:39:08,160 --> 00:39:09,700
I'm sure they became quite worried,

458
00:39:09,701 --> 00:39:13,440
and alarms began going off across the country, in fact.

459
00:39:14,400 --> 00:39:19,300
At 9.52 a.m., the FAA summoned Air Force chase planes.

460
00:39:21,680 --> 00:39:26,460
NF-16 caught up to the derelict aircraft, 45,000 feet over Memphis.

461
00:39:26,800 --> 00:39:30,420
They noticed no gaping holes in the airplane,

462
00:39:30,800 --> 00:39:35,060
none of these windows blown out, for instance, no holes in the windscreen.

463
00:39:35,061 --> 00:39:42,600
And no response from the target aircraft as I mentioned, both sides of the cockpit are condensed or frosted over.

464
00:39:43,460 --> 00:39:45,760
Could not see in the cockpit area.

465
00:39:47,640 --> 00:39:52,160
Based on what the pilots observed, it was likely that the cabin had depressurized

466
00:39:52,161 --> 00:39:56,900
and the inhabitants were incapacitated and quite probably dead.

467
00:39:59,520 --> 00:40:04,560
With no radio contact in over an hour and no idea what was happening on board,

468
00:40:05,060 --> 00:40:10,280
All anyone could do was watch and wait for the Learjet to run out of fuel and crash.

469
00:40:11,700 --> 00:40:15,280
The Air Force and the FAA really did not have a lot of options.

470
00:40:15,820 --> 00:40:18,160
Again, you have to remember this was well before 9-11,

471
00:40:18,360 --> 00:40:22,580
so there was undoubtedly no great concern about terrorism or anything like that.

472
00:40:23,300 --> 00:40:28,200
The aircraft was on a steady course over the vast Midwest stretches of this country.

473
00:40:28,820 --> 00:40:33,000
It wasn't going to overfly any large cities or anything, so the options were quite limited.

474
00:40:33,001 --> 00:40:39,300
The aircraft was escorted the entire way up north until the engines quit and it crashed.

475
00:40:45,540 --> 00:40:50,760
NTSB investigators toured the twisted wreckage at the crash site near Aberdeen, South Dakota,

476
00:40:51,540 --> 00:40:54,820
over a thousand miles from the flight's intended destination.

477
00:40:56,680 --> 00:41:01,380
The evidence indicated that the plane did indeed suffer a massive loss of cabin pressure.

478
00:41:01,381 --> 00:41:07,360
Aircraft such as the Lear and airliners that people fly around in today are pressurized.

479
00:41:07,620 --> 00:41:11,340
That's because they fly so high that the air is too thin to sustain life.

480
00:41:12,460 --> 00:41:17,720
And aircraft itself compresses air to put into the cockpit and the cabin.

481
00:41:18,180 --> 00:41:22,340
And again, people need compressed air, a lower atmospheric pressure to live, actually.

482
00:41:24,000 --> 00:41:27,480
Outside air flows into the engine and passes through a compressor.

483
00:41:27,481 --> 00:41:32,480
The air is pumped through filters and then circulates throughout the cabin.

484
00:41:32,481 --> 00:41:42,480
An outflow valve at the back of the plane lets some of the air out so that a cabin pressure of about 6,000 feet can be maintained.

485
00:41:42,481 --> 00:41:50,480
A remnant of another valve in the system, the flow control valve, was retrieved from the crash site.

486
00:41:50,481 --> 00:41:51,181
site.

487
00:41:51,182 --> 00:41:56,000
Found in the fully closed position, it would have prevented airflow into the cabin,

488
00:41:56,001 --> 00:42:02,560
causing depressurization. Also found in the debris was the cockpit voice recorder.

489
00:42:02,561 --> 00:42:03,261
While

490
00:42:03,262 --> 00:42:09,200
no actual voices were audible, investigators could hear the cabin altitude warning alarm.

491
00:42:09,201 --> 00:42:15,720
The theory was that the cabin had depressurized very rapidly, making it difficult for the

492
00:42:15,721 --> 00:42:16,741
crew to react.

493
00:42:16,742 --> 00:42:22,720
At their altitude, those on board could have become hypoxic and lost consciousness

494
00:42:22,840 --> 00:42:27,160
in as little as 15 seconds.

495
00:42:27,161 --> 00:42:31,680
Hypoxia is a physiological condition where the brain simply does not get enough oxygen

496
00:42:31,681 --> 00:42:35,496
to function correctly. There are varying degrees of hypoxia.

497
00:42:35,497 --> 00:42:37,340
You can become mildly hypoxic

498
00:42:37,341 --> 00:42:42,557
driving up in Denver, Colorado if you're not used to that type of an altitude.

499
00:42:42,558 --> 00:42:43,560
Severe hypoxia,

500
00:42:43,561 --> 00:42:47,240
though can render you unconscious and of course eventually it can kill you.

501
00:42:49,720 --> 00:42:54,540
The older private jet models were not equipped with an automatic pressurization system that

502
00:42:54,541 --> 00:42:59,218
activates as soon as a loss of cabin pressure is detected.

503
00:42:59,219 --> 00:43:01,880
Unlike the primary pressurization

504
00:43:01,881 --> 00:43:07,120
system, which uses compressed air from the engine, the emergency system relies on oxygen

505
00:43:07,121 --> 00:43:10,400
from a reserve tank, commonly found in the nose of the plane.

506
00:43:12,760 --> 00:43:17,600
Not having the automatic system makes the routine pre-flight check of oxygen systems

507
00:43:17,601 --> 00:43:18,900
that much more vital.

508
00:43:19,460 --> 00:43:23,900
Prior to any flight, the oxygen system should be checked out thoroughly

509
00:43:23,901 --> 00:43:28,800
because it is your lifeline in the event of an emergency depressurization.

510
00:43:29,740 --> 00:43:34,280
So prior to any flight, the crew should check the oxygen mass that is flowing.

511
00:43:34,780 --> 00:43:36,280
Here's the emergency position.

512
00:43:36,281 --> 00:43:37,460
You can hear the mask.

513
00:43:38,920 --> 00:43:41,500
And they should put the mask on and breathe.

514
00:43:46,520 --> 00:43:53,500
All small jet aircraft designed after 1969 are equipped with an automatic emergency pressurization system.

515
00:43:54,220 --> 00:43:56,300
The Learjet in question was designed earlier.

516
00:43:56,880 --> 00:44:04,020
Since the older models are still flying, the NTSB has suggested the FAA require retrofitting them with the automatic system.

517
00:44:04,021 --> 00:44:11,020
This accident was a particular concern to the safety board and I think to the air taxi

518
00:44:11,700 --> 00:44:18,700
operators because this particular corner of the industry is growing almost exponentially.

519
00:44:18,701 --> 00:44:25,260
Although the FAA acknowledges that the presence of an automatic pressurization system would

520
00:44:25,261 --> 00:44:28,660
enhance safety, it has not implemented this proposal.

